{"name":"Green Bay","hex":"#7e9285","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Green Bay","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place in Canada:\nA bay in northern Newfoundland in Newfoundland and Labrador.\nA bay in Labrador in Newfoundland and Labrador.\nA community in Lunenburg County, Nova Scotia.\nA community on Manitoulin Island, Ontario.","A bay in northern Newfoundland in Newfoundland and Labrador."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Green_Bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green Bay (Lake Michigan)","blocks":[{"text":"Green Bay is an arm of Lake Michigan, located along the south coast of Michigan's Upper Peninsula and the east coast of Wisconsin. It is separated from the rest of the lake by the Door Peninsula in Wisconsin, the Garden Peninsula in Michigan, and the chain of islands between them, all formed by the Niagara Escarpment. Green Bay is some 120 miles (193 km) long, with a width ranging from about 10 to 20 miles ; it is 1,626 square miles (4,210 km2) in area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Greenbay.svg/330px-Greenbay.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Bay_(Lake_Michigan)","wikidata":"Q947086"},{"title":"Green Bay, Newfoundland and Labrador","blocks":[{"text":"Green Bay is the name of two bays in the Canadian province of Newfoundland and Labrador."},{"items":["On Newfoundland, Green Bay is a natural bay located on the eastern side of the Baie Verte Peninsula. Communities located in Green Bay are: Burlington, Harry's Harbour, Jackson's Cove, King's Point, Little Bay, Middle Arm, Nicky's Nose Cove, Rattling Brook and Smith's Harbour. In 1907, Newfoundland's Premier, Sir Robert Bond proposed an ocean line connection between Killary Harbour in Ireland and Green Bay in Newfoundland.","On the south coast of Labrador, Green Bay is a naturally wooded bay on the north shore of the Strait of Belle Isle."]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Bay%2C_Newfoundland_and_Labrador","wikidata":"Q5602311"},{"title":"Bay","blocks":[{"text":"A bay is a recessed, coastal body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an ocean, a lake, or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, sea, sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A fjord is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.\nThe term embayment is also used for related features, such as extinct bays or freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg/330px-Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay","wikidata":"Q39594"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_16%2C_1972%2C_cropped_from_AS16-118-18879.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_16%2C_1972%2C_cropped_from_AS16-118-18879.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_16%2C_1972%2C_cropped_from_AS16-118-18879.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_14%2C_2022%2C_rotated_and_cropped_from_ISS067-E-14016.png/500px-Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_14%2C_2022%2C_rotated_and_cropped_from_ISS067-E-14016.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_14%2C_2022%2C_rotated_and_cropped_from_ISS067-E-14016.png/500px-Green_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_14%2C_2022%2C_rotated_and_cropped_from_ISS067-E-14016.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Bay_in_Lake_Michigan_on_April_14%2C_2022%2C_rotated_and_cropped_from_ISS067-E-14016.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/20040723_Tall_Ships_Boating_08_Small_Web_view.jpg/250px-20040723_Tall_Ships_Boating_08_Small_Web_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/20040723_Tall_Ships_Boating_08_Small_Web_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca